@*
    One part of the empire, opened over the card that showed it.

    The lite editor's whole shape: the card says what the empire looks like, and pressing any of it
    opens the editor for that one thing here. What goes inside is the same component the sections use
    — the flag picker, the room picker, the trait picker — so there is one editor for each thing and
    this only decides where it sits.

    Not ConfirmDialog, and not a fork of it. That one is sized for a question: a 30rem sheet that
    grows with its content and never scrolls, which is right for two sentences and hopeless for three
    hundred emblems. The parts worth having are the same though, and are taken from it deliberately —
    the backdrop, the focus on open, Escape, and the stopPropagation that keeps a press on a button
    from also reading as a press on the backdrop behind it.
*@

<div class="sem-modal sem-editor-modal @(Compact ? "sem-editor-compact" : null)" @onclick="Close">
    <div class="sheet" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="@_titleId"
         tabindex="-1" @ref="_sheet" @onkeydown="OnKey" @onclick:stopPropagation="true">

        <div class="head">
            <h2 id="@_titleId">@Title</h2>

            @*
                A cross as well as the Done below, because a dialog this size is closed from wherever
                the eye happens to be and the foot of a scrolled editor is a long way from the top.
            *@
            <button type="button" class="icon" title="Close" aria-label="Close" @onclick="Close">
                <ActionIcon Name="close" />
            </button>
        </div>

        @*
            The scrolling part, and the reason this component exists. Everything the editors do —
            their own scroll lists, their sticky heads — happens inside this.
        *@
        <div class="body">
            @ChildContent
        </div>

        <div class="choices">
            @*
                Done rather than Save or OK. Nothing here is being confirmed: the edits went into the
                design as they were made, exactly as they do in the sections, and the Save button on
                the page behind is still the only thing that writes anything.
            *@
            <button type="button" class="primary" @onclick="Close">Done</button>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    private ElementReference _sheet;

    /// <summary>Ties the sheet to its heading for a screen reader, uniquely on a page.</summary>
    private readonly string _titleId = $"editor-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";

    /// <summary>What is being edited, named as the section that holds it is named.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required string Title { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The editor itself.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required RenderFragment ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Called when the dialog is closed, however it was closed.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnClose { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether this holds a form rather than a picker.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Three fields do not want a sixty-four-rem sheet with a scrolling middle. A compact one is
    /// narrow, sized to its content, and - the part that matters - does not clip: a ComboBox drops
    /// its list as an absolutely positioned child, and the scrolling body would cut it off exactly
    /// as it once did inside a section.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Compact { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Takes the keyboard when the dialog appears, so Escape reaches it and Tab stays inside.
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender)
        {
            await _sheet.FocusAsync();
        }
    }

    private Task OnKey(KeyboardEventArgs e) =>
        e.Key == "Escape" ? Close() : Task.CompletedTask;

    private Task Close() => OnClose.InvokeAsync();
}
